Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Riemannian Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Riemannian infrageometry measures and describes a discrete substrate \
represented by a graph ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["G", "TI"], "\[LongEqual]", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["V", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["E", "TI"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "278bc60a-62d9-43ce-a978-4eed72c62618"],
 ". Euclidean infrageometry, in contrast, is synthetic geometry: \
constructions with natural objects (points, segments, lines, circles) in the \
substrate."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Classical geometry has infinitesimality: a segment can be subdivided \
infinitely many times, whatever the scale of the observer. A graph has no \
infinitely small part. Infrageometry aggregates instead. An observer scale ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["r", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "51b336cf-c5bf-4905-b474-fe13cab33a6d"],
 ", the number of steps the observer inspects at once, must be given before \
an object or a measurement makes sense."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"3ad4df07-4464-4d9e-ad46-cab3223d9af3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The claim of infrageometry is that limits of graphs are enough to capture \
the geometry of a surface, although a graph describes only points and a web \
of paths between them. This is close to Riemann's original approach. Riemann \
looked for the infinitesimal object that captures the essence of measuring \
the length of a curve, and found the line element ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["s", "TI"], "2"], "\[LongEqual]", 
    SubscriptBox[
     StyleBox["g", "TI"], 
     StyleBox[
      RowBox[{"i", "j"}], "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["i", "TI"]], 
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    SuperscriptBox[
     StyleBox["x", "TI"], 
     StyleBox["j", "TI"]]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "d1b5afe4-7e19-4dfb-8305-577005f60c01"],
 "."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"0846c390-be27-4212-9e1b-f93bb13b7f05"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In synthetic geometry on a graph a construction is not unique; it is \
multi-valued. Two vertices are in general joined by many shortest paths, and \
so they have many midpoints. We deal with this by enumerating the objects a \
construction gives (points in dimension 0, paths in dimension 1) and \
attaching to the construction its vertex density ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]", ":", 
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"0", ",", "1", ",", "2", ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], "}"}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "98789844-885b-4b55-a3df-9bad22619445"],
 ", where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Rho]", "(", 
    StyleBox["v", "TI"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b65a3647-75c9-43bf-aace-c10d471eaa4e"],
 " is the number of objects through the vertex ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["v", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "ff09d5d1-5cfd-40f5-ac5f-60d2f5974c7d"],
 ". Let graphs converge to a space, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
      SubscriptBox[
       StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]], ",", 
      SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", 
       StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], ")"}], "\[Rule]", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["M", "TI"], ",", 
      StyleBox["d", "TI"], ",", "\[Mu]"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1a311134-0555-469c-a903-9df0ae076919"],
 ", in the Gromov-Hausdorff sense for metric measure spaces, with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox[
    StyleBox["d", "TI"], 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "77d6e12c-af38-4e0d-87ec-3f4916d5ce33"],
 " the rescaled path metric and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Mu]", 
    StyleBox["n", "TI"]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4b3b37c0-9731-405d-8581-46a41f4a2e4b"],
 " the rescaled counting measure. We imagine that the normalized densities ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "/", 
    UnderscriptBox["\[Sum]", 
     StyleBox["v", "TI"],
     LimitsPositioning->True], 
    SubscriptBox["\[Rho]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     StyleBox["v", "TI"], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "25b8481b-f890-4147-a038-705aea5b1604"],
 " then converge weakly to the Dirac density of the corresponding object in \
the infinitesimal world, concentrated on a point or along a curve."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"e9ebc354-265e-44af-8a4e-169ddce93d56"],

Cell["\<\
The Riemannian branch measures the substrate instead of constructing on it. \
Its objects are the level sets of the distance from a vertex: the closed ball \
B_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) <= r} and the shell S_r(c) = {v : d(c, v) = r}, each an \
inert head, InfraBall and InfraShell, whose volumes InfraMeasurement reads \
and whose vertices FindInfraRepresentative gives; FindInfraShell computes the \
shell as a sorted vertex list. A geodesic is read at a scale: the observer \
sees s steps back, and a walk is a geodesic at infra-scale s when every \
window of s consecutive vertices together with the next one is a shortest \
path, so scale 1 is any walk and scale Infinity is a segment; \
FindInfraGeodesic grows them, and SegmentGraph and SprayGraph hold every \
geodesic between two vertices, or from one vertex, as a directed acyclic \
graph whose directed paths are exactly the geodesics, built from the distance \
matrix without listing them. The measurements are counts: the ball volume \
V(r) = |B_r|, the shell area A(r) = V(r) - V(r - 1) and the tube volume T(s) \
= |{w : d(w, core) <= s}|, each under a chosen convention for its outermost \
layer. The log-difference quotient q(r) = d log V / d log r is affine in r(r \
+ 1) over a middle window of radii, and a regression there reads the \
dimension as the intercept and the scalar curvature as the slope, \
Bishop-Gromov on a graph; the ball and the shell are two independent probes \
of the same pair, and the tube about a geodesic reads R + Ric(v, v) along its \
core. The metric tensor at p is the matrix d(p, u) / d(p, v) with u the foot \
of v on the interval I(p, w): on a substrate whose large-scale metric is \
round it tends to max(0, cos theta), the Gram matrix of the unit directions \
at p, and on a periodic tiling to the projection tensor of the polyhedral \
norm the path metric converges to. The Riemann tensor is not here yet. The \
ball, tube, cylinder, cone and sphere heads and the two geodesic graphs are \
on the Euclidean Infrageometry guide, the covering dimension and the ball \
intersection complex on the Infra Topology guide, the tangent and cotangent \
spaces on the Infra Analysis guide, and the Levi-Civita connection on the \
Infra Fiber Bundles guide.\
\>", "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"920f429b-9df8-4a42-8a09-726c5286c933"],

